WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.348
Lesson 2.3: Digital Choreography and Drama-Dance.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.623
GENAI 101 - Module 2: Theater.

3
00:00:08.623 --> 00:00:15.172
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:21.113
In this lesson you'll choreograph a short movement piece
from generated video.

5
00:00:21.113 --> 00:00:37.959
You'll write a movement score of four phrases, generate each
phrase, keep a log of what failed and how you fixed it,
assemble the phrases with a choreographic device, and write
a note about how the piece would be taught to live dancers
or actors.

6
00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:39.602
Expect the tool to fail.

7
00:00:39.602 --> 00:00:42.752
That's why this lesson builds problem solving.

8
00:00:43.352 --> 00:00:55.113
Text-to-video models respond to the same specificity that
music and text models do, and dance educators already have
the vocabulary.

9
00:00:55.113 --> 00:00:58.231
Body: which parts lead, what shape?

10
00:00:58.231 --> 00:01:00.637
Action: the movement verbs.

11
00:01:00.637 --> 00:01:04.111
Space: level, direction, pathway, size.

12
00:01:04.111 --> 00:01:06.784
Time: tempo, rhythm, duration.

13
00:01:06.784 --> 00:01:09.992
Energy: the quality of the movement.

14
00:01:09.992 --> 00:01:28.791
A prompt that names all five, like 'one dancer, arms leading
a slow backward spiral at low level, heavy and bound, then a
sudden light reach upward and freeze, five seconds', gives
the model something to render.

15
00:01:28.791 --> 00:01:30.840
'A sad dance' does not.

16
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.067
Text-to-video is the least reliable tool in this course.

17
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:41.519
Bodies drift, hands multiply, and the model may ignore your
tempo.

18
00:01:41.519 --> 00:01:46.228
For every unusable clip, name the failure and pick a fix.

19
00:01:46.228 --> 00:01:55.233
First, simplify the prompt: one dancer, one action, one
quality, a single continuous shot, a stated duration.

20
00:01:55.233 --> 00:02:06.139
Second, change the tool or the mode; try image-to-video from
a still you make, or switch to character animation for
precise control.

21
00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:14.152
Third, edit the output: trim to the two usable seconds, slow
it down, mirror it, cut on the beat.

22
00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:21.588
Your failure-and-fix log is part of the submission, because
the diagnosis is the learning.

23
00:02:22.188 --> 00:02:24.016
The model makes phrases.

24
00:02:24.016 --> 00:02:25.464
You make the piece.

25
00:02:25.464 --> 00:02:36.360
Choreographic devices like repetition, canon, contrast, and
accumulation are decisions about sequence and relationship,
and they belong to you.

26
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.237
Four generated phrases arranged with intention is a
composition.

27
00:02:41.237 --> 00:02:45.276
The same four phrases dumped in order is a slideshow.

28
00:02:45.276 --> 00:02:50.533
Choose your device before you generate, and let it shape the
prompts.

29
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:53.063
Finish with a transfer note.

30
00:02:53.063 --> 00:03:05.055
In three sentences, describe how you'd teach these four
phrases to live dancers or actors in a rehearsal room, and
what the video showed you that improvisation wouldn't have.

31
00:03:05.055 --> 00:03:14.359
When you reflect, think about the conversations this
activity would require in a classroom: safety, consent, and
body image among them.

32
00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:26.902
Then export with title cards naming each phrase, post it
with your score and your log, and tell two classmates which
device you see in their piece and how it could be performed
live.
